{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-39:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The request depends on God as the source of guidance: when He lets someone stray, no other guide remains.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:36\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يُضْلِلِ ٱللَّهُ فَمَا لَهُۥ مِنْ هَادٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The community asks God directly to guide it, and this verse names the consequence of His withholding guidance.\",\"limits\":\"It states that no guide can aid one whom God leads astray; it does not say that petitioners are in that state.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:37\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God's guidance leaves no rival able to misdirect its recipient, giving the request for the path an assurance of protection as well as direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:37\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يَهْدِ ٱللَّهُ فَمَا لَهُۥ مِن مُّضِلٍّ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks God to guide the praying community; the parallel conditional makes the security of that guidance explicit.\",\"limits\":\"This verse describes God's guidance in general and does not identify its recipients with the speakers of the Fatiha.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-40:38\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"A human believer invites his people to follow him so he can guide them to the path of right judgment; this portrays guidance as arriving through a caller and being met by following.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"40:38\",\"ar\":\"ٱتَّبِعُونِ أَهْدِكُمْ سَبِيلَ ٱلرَّشَادِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses join a form of هدى with a named way, while the surrounding Fatiha addresses God for guidance.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not name a human intermediary, and سبيل الرشاد is not the same wording as الصراط المستقيم.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-41:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to turn upright toward God and seek His forgiveness shares the focus's q-w-m uprightness field, making the straight path an orientation toward Him as well as a route.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"41:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱسْتَقِيمُوا۟ إِلَيْهِ وَٱسْتَغْفِرُوهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names an upright path; this passage directs people to be upright toward God.\",\"limits\":\"The passage uses an imperative verb and does not itself call that orientation a path.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-41:17\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Thamud are said to have preferred blindness over guidance after they were guided; this makes the requested guidance consequential because people may reject it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"41:17\",\"ar\":\"فَهَدَيْنَٰهُمْ فَٱسْتَحَبُّوا۟ ٱلْعَمَىٰ عَلَى ٱلْهُدَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's petition for guidance is heard against a scene where guidance was given but blindness was preferred.\",\"limits\":\"This is a particular account of Thamud and does not say that their choice is the condition of the Fatiha's speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:26\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The statement that God responds to believers and increases them from His favor might place the guidance petition within divine response and added favor.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"42:26\",\"ar\":\"وَيَسْتَجِيبُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَيَزِيدُهُم مِّن فَضْلِهِۦ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"missing\":\"This passage does not identify its response as an answer to supplication or name guidance as what is increased; an ayah linking divine response to a request for guidance could establish that connection.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:52\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Revelation is made light by which God guides, and the Messenger guides to a straight path; the requested path is thus linked to revealed guidance conveyed through a Messenger.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"42:52\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن جَعَلْنَٰهُ نُورًۭا نَّهْدِى بِهِۦ مَن نَّشَآءُ مِنْ عِبَادِنَا\"},{\"ref\":\"42:52\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّكَ لَتَهْدِىٓ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The passage contains the same guidance and straight-path wording as the focus, while explaining that guidance comes through revealed light.\",\"limits\":\"The focus asks God in the first-person plural; this passage distinguishes God's guiding by revelation from the Messenger's guidance to the path.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-43:10\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The earth is made a dwelling with plural routes so people may find guidance; this sets the focus's singular, definite path alongside a created landscape of ways.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"43:10\",\"ar\":\"وَجَعَلَ لَكُمْ فِيهَا سُبُلًۭا لَّعَلَّكُمْ تَهْتَدُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses join paths with guidance, but the focus asks for one specified path while 43:10 speaks of plural routes through the earth.\",\"limits\":\"43:10 concerns routes made in the earth and does not state that all its plural ways are morally equivalent to the straight path.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-43:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Messenger is told to hold fast to revelation because he is already on a straight path; the focus's petition can be heard as seeking the orientation that revelation establishes and sustains.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"43:43\",\"ar\":\"فَٱسْتَمْسِكْ بِٱلَّذِىٓ أُوحِىَ إِلَيْكَ\"},{\"ref\":\"43:43\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّكَ عَلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact straight-path formula appears alongside a command to cling to what was revealed.\",\"limits\":\"The verse addresses the Messenger as already on the path; it does not say that the focus's speakers have the same status or role.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-43:57\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-43:64\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Jesus identifies worship of the shared Lord as a straight path, giving content to the focus's request immediately after its declaration of exclusive worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"43:64\",\"ar\":\"فَٱعْبُدُوهُ ۚ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌۭ مُّسْتَقِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha moves from exclusive worship and seeking help to asking for the straight path.\",\"limits\":\"The demonstrative هَٰذَا refers to the command in its own passage; the parallel does not make the two passages identical in setting.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-44:26\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-46:13\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Those who affirm God as Lord and then remain upright receive a promise of security; the focus's straight-path request can include sustained fidelity after initial orientation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"46:13\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ قَالُوا۟ رَبُّنَا ٱللَّهُ ثُمَّ ٱسْتَقَٰمُوا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The adjective المستقيم in the focus and the verb استقاموا share the uprightness root, while both passages address orientation toward God.\",\"limits\":\"46:13 describes steadfast people and gives no explicit path image; it does not say that they are asking for guidance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-46:30\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Qur'an is described as guiding to truth and to an upright way, a direct parallel that joins the focus's path to both revelation and truth.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"46:30\",\"ar\":\"يَهْدِىٓ إِلَى ٱلْحَقِّ وَإِلَىٰ طَرِيقٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The jinn who heard the Qur'an report its guidance to a طريق مستقيم, matching the focus's request for a صراط مستقيم.\",\"limits\":\"The wording differs between طريق and صراط, and this passage reports the listeners' account rather than a first-person prayer.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-47:17\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Those already guided are given more guidance, so the focus's request need not be limited to people who have never received direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"47:17\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ ٱهْتَدَوْا۟ زَادَهُمْ هُدًۭى\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for guidance in the plural; this verse explicitly says that guided people receive an increase of it.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not describe what additional guidance consists of or equate it with the straight path.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-47:24\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The sequence links failure to ponder the Qur'an with hearts under locks and then with turning back after guidance becomes clear; the requested path includes receptive engagement with revelation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"47:24\",\"ar\":\"أَفَلَا يَتَدَبَّرُونَ ٱلْقُرْءَانَ أَمْ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبٍ أَقْفَالُهَآ\"},{\"ref\":\"47:25\",\"ar\":\"مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلْهُدَى\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The adjacent verse names guidance that had become clear, while the focus asks God to guide the community.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not specify pondering the Qur'an as the means of guidance; the sequence describes a different audience and response.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-48:2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Messenger is guided to a straight path amid forgiveness and the completion of favor; this frames guidance as a divine favor that can accompany an already unfolding mission.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"48:2\",\"ar\":\"وَيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكَ وَيَهْدِيَكَ صِرَٰطًۭا مُّسْتَقِيمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The path wording is an exact lexical parallel, and the focus likewise requests guidance from God.\",\"limits\":\"48:2 addresses the Messenger with an indefinite path noun; the focus is a communal request using the definite noun.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-48:20\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Guidance to a straight path follows a protected communal victory, presenting historical events and signs as part of how a community is guided.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"48:20\",\"ar\":\"وَكَفَّ أَيْدِىَ ٱلنَّاسِ عَنكُمْ وَلِتَكُونَ ءَايَةًۭ لِّلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَيَهْدِيَكُمْ صِرَٰطًۭا مُّسْتَقِيمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse directly repeats guidance to a straight path and places it alongside divine protection and a sign for believers.\",\"limits\":\"The verse ties this guidance to a specific communal event; it does not define every instance of the requested path by that event.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-49:17\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Guidance to faith is named as God's favor, not as something recipients may claim as their own merit; the petition can therefore be heard as asking for a gift.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"49:17\",\"ar\":\"بَلِ ٱللَّهُ يَمُنُّ عَلَيْكُمْ أَنْ هَدَىٰكُمْ لِلْإِيمَٰنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared هدى root connects the focus's imperative request to this explicit account of God granting guidance.\",\"limits\":\"The verse specifies guidance to faith and rebukes a particular claim by the addressed people; it does not describe the focus's speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-50:1\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-51:32\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-52:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-53:23\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage contrasts conjecture and desire with guidance that came from the Lord, so the requested path stands against self-made religious claims.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"53:23\",\"ar\":\"إِن يَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا ٱلظَّنَّ وَمَا تَهْوَى ٱلْأَنفُسُ\"},{\"ref\":\"53:23\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ جَآءَهُم مِّن رَّبِّهِمُ ٱلْهُدَىٰٓ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for guidance from God, while 53:23 names guidance from the Lord against conjecture and desire.\",\"limits\":\"This passage concerns specific claims about named deities; it does not identify the focus's speakers with those claimants.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-53:30\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse contrasts straying from God's way with being guided, giving a direct negative and positive counterpart to the requested path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"53:30\",\"ar\":\"بِمَن ضَلَّ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦ وَهُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَنِ ٱهْتَدَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages couple guidance with a way, and the focus's plea asks to be among the guided rather than those straying from it.\",\"limits\":\"53:30 calls it God's سبيل and does not use الصراط المستقيم.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-55:9\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The command to establish the measure in justice uses the q-w-m root family of المستقيم; this offers an association between uprightness and equitable public measure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"55:9\",\"ar\":\"وَأَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلْوَزْنَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ وَلَا تُخْسِرُوا۟ ٱلْمِيزَانَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared root and explicit justice language invite the path's uprightness to be heard alongside fair dealing.\",\"limits\":\"55:9 does not call the measure a path or equate social justice with the full meaning of المستقيم.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-57:10\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Spending and fighting are explicitly placed in God's way; this supplies a concrete communal practice that can belong to the broad path sought in the Fatiha.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"57:9\",\"ar\":\"لِّيُخْرِجَكُم مِّنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ\"},{\"ref\":\"57:10\",\"ar\":\"أَلَّا تُنفِقُوا۟ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The nearby movement from darkness to light and the phrase سبيل الله place a path of commitment after divine illumination.\",\"limits\":\"This passage names particular acts and does not call them the straight path or make them an exhaustive definition of it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-62:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The parable of people who carry scripture without carrying it out ends by saying God does not guide wrongdoers; possession of revealed words alone is not the same as being guided.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"62:5\",\"ar\":\"حُمِّلُوا۟ ٱلتَّوْرَىٰةَ ثُمَّ لَمْ يَحْمِلُوهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"62:5\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ لَا يَهْدِى ٱلْقَوْمَ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks God to guide, while this verse distinguishes bearing scripture from the guidance that shapes conduct.\",\"limits\":\"The parable and its judgment concern the people addressed in 62:5; it does not assign the same failure to the Fatiha's speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-62:11\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"People disperse toward trade and leave the Prophet standing; the image of him قائم offers a q-w-m root echo, while their departure depicts a competing orientation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"62:11\",\"ar\":\"ٱنفَضُّوٓا۟ إِلَيْهَا وَتَرَكُوكَ قَآئِمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's upright-path adjective and the verse's standing figure share a root family, set against people turning away.\",\"limits\":\"قائم describes the Prophet's posture here and does not establish that the focus's path concerns this incident.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-64:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Rejectors object that human beings guide them, then disbelieve and turn away; this shows that guidance may arrive through human messengers and be rejected for that reason.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"64:6\",\"ar\":\"فَقَالُوا۟ أَبَشَرٌۭ يَهْدُونَنَا\"},{\"ref\":\"64:6\",\"ar\":\"فَكَفَرُوا۟ وَتَوَلَّوا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks God for guidance, while this passage exposes resistance to guidance mediated by messengers.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not name a human intermediary; the passage reports the rejectors' objection rather than endorsing it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-65:2\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Just witnesses are commanded to establish testimony for God; its إقامة alongside عدل echoes the focus's uprightness as a possible public and legal quality.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"65:2\",\"ar\":\"وَأَشْهِدُوا۟ ذَوَىْ عَدْلٍۢ مِّنكُمْ وَأَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلشَّهَٰدَةَ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The uprightness root appears in a command joined directly to just witnesses and testimony for God.\",\"limits\":\"The legal command does not explicitly call its order a path or define the focus's path by this procedure.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-67:22\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"One person walks face-down while another walks evenly on a straight path; this makes straightness a visible manner of movement, not just an abstract destination.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"67:22\",\"ar\":\"أَفَمَن يَمْشِى مُكِبًّا عَلَىٰ وَجْهِهِۦٓ أَهْدَىٰٓ أَمَّن يَمْشِى سَوِيًّا عَلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse repeats the straight-path wording and sets it against a bodily image of disoriented movement.\",\"limits\":\"67:22 poses a comparison in its own argument; it does not explicitly say that the Fatiha's speakers are either walker.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-68:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God knows those who stray from His way and those who are guided; the requested route belongs within a distinction whose full knowledge is God's.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"68:7\",\"ar\":\"بِمَن ضَلَّ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦ وَهُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِٱلْمُهْتَدِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks God for the path; the verse places straying and guidance under God's knowledge.\",\"limits\":\"68:7 does not name الصراط المستقيم and makes no direct statement about the Fatiha's petitioners.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-70:33\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Believers are described as standing by their testimonies; this q-w-m root echo associates uprightness with faithful public witness.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"70:33\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِينَ هُم بِشَهَٰدَٰتِهِمْ قَآئِمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's uprightness adjective meets a description of people maintaining testimony.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not mention guidance or a path, and قَائِمُونَ describes testimony practice in this list of qualities.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-71:1\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-71:5\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Nuh reports to his Lord that he called his people night and day, while the next verse says they fled; this contrasts a human caller's effort with the focus's appeal to God for guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"71:5\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ رَبِّ إِنِّى دَعَوْتُ قَوْمِى لَيْلًۭا وَنَهَارًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"71:6\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمْ يَزِدْهُمْ دُعَآءِىٓ إِلَّا فِرَارًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages involve an appeal addressed to God and a call concerning a people, but the focus asks for divine guidance and Nuh reports his own unsuccessful calling.\",\"limits\":\"Nuh's report is not a request for God to guide his people in these words, and this passage has no path term.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-72:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Remaining upright on the way is presented conditionally, with abundant water following and a test in the next verse; the requested path entails continued adherence with consequences.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"72:16\",\"ar\":\"وَأَلَّوِ ٱسْتَقَٰمُوا۟ عَلَى ٱلطَّرِيقَةِ لَأَسْقَيْنَٰهُم مَّآءً غَدَقًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"72:17\",\"ar\":\"لِّنَفْتِنَهُمْ فِيهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"This passage combines the uprightness root with being on a way, directly echoing the focus's straight path.\",\"limits\":\"Its conditional promise concerns the addressed people and is immediately qualified by the test; it does not define the focus's path as material abundance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-73:6\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Night rising is described as more upright or firmer in speech; the q-w-m root offers an association between the focus's straightness and disciplined recitation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"73:6\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ نَاشِئَةَ ٱلَّيْلِ هِىَ أَشَدُّ وَطْـًۭٔا وَأَقْوَمُ قِيلًا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The root of المستقيم appears in a description of night worship and speech, close to the Fatiha's setting as recited prayer.\",\"limits\":\"أَقْوَمُ qualifies speech in this passage; it does not say that recitation is the path named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-73:20\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The passage joins night prayer, Qur'an recitation, and the command to establish prayer; this offers a ritual setting in which the Fatiha's path petition is repeatedly voiced.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"73:20\",\"ar\":\"فَٱقْرَءُوا۟ مَا تَيَسَّرَ مِنَ ٱلْقُرْءَانِ\"},{\"ref\":\"73:20\",\"ar\":\"وَأَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha is recited in salah, and this passage connects recitation with establishing prayer.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not mention the Fatiha or its path request, and its prayer instructions address a different setting.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-74:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-75:29\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"As death approaches, the legs entwine and the next verse says the person's driving is to the Lord; this can contrast a life spent seeking a chosen path with an unavoidable final passage.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"75:29\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلْتَفَّتِ ٱلسَّاقُ بِٱلسَّاقِ\"},{\"ref\":\"75:30\",\"ar\":\"إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ يَوْمَئِذٍ ٱلْمَسَاقُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for direction, while the adjacent death scene names the destination and compelled movement at life's end.\",\"limits\":\"The death passage uses مساق rather than guidance or path language; the contrast between chosen guidance and compelled arrival is an interpretation of the sequence.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-77:37\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-78:36\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-78:39\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse says whoever wills may take a return to his Lord; the requested straight path can be heard in relation to that chosen destination.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"78:39\",\"ar\":\"فَمَن شَآءَ ٱتَّخَذَ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ مَـَٔابًا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages frame orientation toward God, with one asking for the way and the other naming a chosen return.\",\"limits\":\"78:39 does not use guidance or path vocabulary, and it emphasizes human willing in a way the focus's petition does not specify.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-81:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The question where people are going is followed by the Qur'an as a reminder and by the possibility of becoming upright; the focus's path petition is one answer to that question of direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"81:26\",\"ar\":\"فَأَيْنَ تَذْهَبُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"81:27\",\"ar\":\"إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا ذِكْرٌۭ لِّلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"81:28\",\"ar\":\"لِمَن شَآءَ مِنكُمْ أَن يَسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The immediate sequence moves from a question about direction to Qur'anic reminder and uprightness, closely meeting the focus's request.\",\"limits\":\"The rhetorical question is not itself a path instruction, and verse 29 further qualifies human willing by God's will.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-81:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The wish to become upright is bounded by God's will in the next verse; the focus's request is a human appeal for an orientation that remains dependent on God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"81:28\",\"ar\":\"لِمَن شَآءَ مِنكُمْ أَن يَسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"81:29\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا تَشَآءُونَ إِلَّآ أَن يَشَآءَ ٱللَّهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's imperative asks for the same uprightness named by يَسْتَقِيمَ, and the adjacent verse places willing under God's will.\",\"limits\":\"The verses distinguish human willing from God's will but do not explicitly describe the focus's speakers or their request.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-86:3\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-87:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God's measured creation is followed by His guiding; this places the focus's request within a wider pattern of divine ordering and direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"87:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ فَسَوَّىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"87:3\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِى قَدَّرَ فَهَدَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared هدى root connects the petition with God's guidance following creation, fashioning, and measure.\",\"limits\":\"87:3 does not specify a moral path or say that its guidance is the same kind requested in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-89:27\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The tranquil soul is told to return to its Lord satisfied and accepted; this gives the requested path an endpoint of return and acceptance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"89:27\",\"ar\":\"يَٰٓأَيَّتُهَا ٱلنَّفْسُ ٱلْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ\"},{\"ref\":\"89:28\",\"ar\":\"ٱرْجِعِىٓ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكِ رَاضِيَةًۭ مَّرْضِيَّةًۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names a path, while this adjacent address names the return to God at its far end.\",\"limits\":\"The passage addresses a tranquil soul at its return and does not explicitly connect that return to the focus's path.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-90:10\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Humanity is shown two guided ways, and the next verse speaks of not venturing through the steep obstacle; this places the requested singular straight path amid choice and a difficult ascent.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"90:10\",\"ar\":\"وَهَدَيْنَٰهُ ٱلنَّجْدَيْنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"90:11\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا ٱقْتَحَمَ ٱلْعَقَبَةَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same guidance root appears with two routes, while the neighboring obstacle image gives the choice a demanding terrain.\",\"limits\":\"90:10 does not name the two ways as good and evil or call either one straight; the relation of the obstacle to them is suggested by sequence.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-92:12\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Guidance is declared to be upon God, which grounds the petition in divine responsibility for showing the way.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"92:12\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ عَلَيْنَا لَلْهُدَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus addresses God with the same guidance root that this verse places upon Him.\",\"limits\":\"The verse's declaration does not specify the form of guidance or promise that every petitioner receives the requested path in the same way.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-93:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The addressee is described as found astray and then guided; this presents guidance as a received change of state and makes repeated asking intelligible even after prior direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"93:7\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَكَ ضَآلًّۭا فَهَدَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for guidance, and this verse pairs the guidance root with its opposite state, ضَالًّا.\",\"limits\":\"The singular addressee's experience is not assigned to every reciter, and ضَالًّا here does not by itself establish moral wrongdoing.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-93:11\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The command to speak of the Lord's favor follows the account of guidance in 93:7; guidance may thus be heard as a favor to acknowledge after it is received.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"93:7\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَكَ ضَآلًّۭا فَهَدَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"93:11\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا بِنِعْمَةِ رَبِّكَ فَحَدِّثْ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The nearby sequence places divine guidance before an instruction to recount the Lord's favor.\",\"limits\":\"93:11 does not specify that guidance is the favor being recounted, and the focus remains a request rather than testimony.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-98:3\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The writings are called qayyimah, bringing the q-w-m family of uprightness from the focus's path into the description of scripture.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"98:3\",\"ar\":\"فِيهَا كُتُبٌۭ قَيِّمَةٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"A word from the same root family describes the contents of the purified sheets in this passage.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not state that these writings are the focus's path or define the adjective's full sense here.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-98:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Sincere worship, prayer, and almsgiving are called دين القيمة; this presents upright religion as enacted practice alongside the focus's request for the upright path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"98:5\",\"ar\":\"لِيَعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ ٱلدِّينَ حُنَفَآءَ وَيُقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَيُؤْتُوا۟ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ\"},{\"ref\":\"98:5\",\"ar\":\"وَذَٰلِكَ دِينُ ٱلْقَيِّمَةِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha declares worship before asking for the path; this verse describes worship and established prayer as دين القيمة.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not explicitly identify that religion with the صراط in the focus, and القيمة has its own grammatical context here.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus is framed by exclusive worship and seeking help before it, then by a phrase identifying the path with those who received favor and distinguishing those under anger and those astray; the request is both dependent and ethically specified.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The adjacent Fatiha verses move from worship and reliance, through a plural petition, to a specification of whose path is sought and which outcomes are excluded.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence supplies the prayer's own framing but does not identify the named groups with particular historical communities.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
